#
# Decoded, with Qt's enum from section 2(b) (TopUp=1, TopDown=2, LeftUp=3, RightUp=4,
# FaceUp=5, FaceDown=6) and the event's value in w0:
#
#   cmp w0,#3 ; b.eq -> case LeftUp(3)      |  b.hi -> check 4
#   cmp w0,#1 ; b.eq -> case TopUp(1)       |  cmp w0,#2 ; b.ne -> UNKNOWN (so 0 is unknown)
#   case 2 (TopDown)  : native==2 ? InvertedLandscape(8) : InvertedPortrait(4)
#   case 4 (RightUp)  : native==2 ? Portrait(1)          : InvertedLandscape(8)
#   case 1 (TopUp)    : native==2 ? Landscape(2)         : Portrait(1)
#   case 3 (LeftUp)   : native==2 ? InvertedPortrait(4)  : Landscape(2)
#   anything else (0, 5 = FaceUp, 6 = FaceDown) -> "() - unknown orientation." and the
#   event is accepted with m_currentOrientation UNCHANGED.
#
# That matches the publicly visible version of the same switch, verbatim:
# src/platforms/mirserver/screen.cpp (ubports/qtmir, focal). Its class is called Screen
# there and PlatformScreen in this build, and the port's build commit is not in the
# public branch list, so the mapping above is quoted from the BINARY -- the source is
# here as corroboration, and it agrees case for case:

void Screen::customEvent(QEvent* event)
{
    OrientationReadingEvent* oReadingEvent = static_cast<OrientationReadingEvent*>(event);
    switch (oReadingEvent->m_orientation) {
        case QOrientationReading::LeftUp: {
            m_currentOrientation = (m_nativeOrientation == Qt::LandscapeOrientation) ?
                        Qt::InvertedPortraitOrientation : Qt::LandscapeOrientation;
            break;
        }
        case QOrientationReading::TopUp: {
            m_currentOrientation = (m_nativeOrientation == Qt::LandscapeOrientation) ?
                        Qt::LandscapeOrientation : Qt::PortraitOrientation;
            break;
        }
        case QOrientationReading::RightUp: {
            m_currentOrientation = (m_nativeOrientation == Qt::LandscapeOrientation) ?
                        Qt::PortraitOrientation : Qt::InvertedLandscapeOrientation;
            break;
        }
        case QOrientationReading::TopDown: {
            m_currentOrientation = (m_nativeOrientation == Qt::LandscapeOrientation) ?
                        Qt::InvertedLandscapeOrientation : Qt::InvertedPortraitOrientation;
            break;
        }
        default: {
            qWarning("Unknown orientation.");
            event->accept();
            return;
        }
#
# The two log strings the binary uses, so the decode above can be matched against the
# device's own journal (doc 70 quoted exactly these lines):
#   0x94b78  "() - unknown orientation."     0x94b98  "() - new orientation="
#   0x94b30  "PlatformScreen["  0x94b40 "]::"  0x94b68 "customEvent"
# and the value logged after "new orientation=" is [x19,#268] = m_currentOrientation,
# i.e. a Qt::ScreenOrientation (1/2/4/8), not the sensorfw number.

-- 6. THE WHOLE CHAIN, END TO END --------------------------------------------------
# nativeOrientation first, because three of the four branches depend on it. It is not
# a sensor reading: PlatformScreen's constructor decides it from the screen geometry
# (llvm-objdump-14 -d --start-address=0x5d150 --stop-address=0x5d194 of the same .so):
#     w0 = (geometry.height() <= geometry.width()) ? 2 : 1     ->  m_nativeOrientation
# and the port's geometry is QRect(0,0 1080x1920) (docs 70 section 1), so
#     m_nativeOrientation = Qt::PortraitOrientation (1)
# which is also what the device logged: "Screen - nativeOrientation is: Qt::PortraitOrientation".
# Then m_currentOrientation starts at Portrait and only changes on an accepted reading.
#
#          sensorfw        ->  QtSensors      ->  qtmir (native portrait)     ->  screen
#   value  name                value  name          result
#   -----  ------------------  -----  -----------   ----------------------------  -------
#     1    LeftUp (left        3     LeftUp        LandscapeOrientation(2)
#          side up)
#     2    RightUp (right      4     RightUp       InvertedLandscapeOrientation(8)
#          side up)
#     3    BottomUp            2     TopDown       InvertedPortraitOrientation(4)
#     4    BottomDown          1     TopUp         PortraitOrientation(1)
#     5    FaceDown            6     FaceDown      (nothing: "unknown orientation.")
#     6    FaceUp              5     FaceUp        (nothing: "unknown orientation.")
#
# Every step of the translation preserves the NAME: LeftUp->LeftUp, RightUp->RightUp,
# BottomUp->TopDown, BottomDown->TopUp, FaceUp->FaceUp, FaceDown->FaceDown. There is no
# inversion anywhere in this chain.
#
# What the names mean physically (orientationinterpreter.cpp, section 4): rotation is
# atan(y/...) in portrait mode and atan(x/...) in landscape mode, and BottomUp vs
# BottomDown is the SIGN of y, LeftUp vs RightUp the sign of x. With this port's
# accelerometer convention -- verified on the device: a flat, screen-up phone reads
# z = +1015 mG (docs 71 section 6), which is the Android/Qt convention where +y points
# to the top of the device -- "upright in portrait" is y > 0, i.e. BottomDown (4), and
# that is the row that yields Qt::PortraitOrientation. Upside down is BottomUp (3) ->
# InvertedPortrait. Both are correct.
#
# AND a flat phone (z = +/-1 g, x ~ y ~ 0) is NOT one of the four top-edge values at
# all: processTopEdge() finds |rotation| below both thresholds, sets topEdge to
# Undefined, and processOrientation() then propagates the FACE value instead -- 6
# (FaceUp) for a screen-up phone. So "6" is the correct, expected value for a flat
# phone, and qtmir ignores it by design.

-- 7. CROSS-CHECK AGAINST WHAT THE DEVICE ITSELF LOGGED (docs 70/71, quoted here
--    because they are the only device-side measurements that exist):
#
#   [ 385.646759] qtmir.sensor: OrientationSensor::readingChanged
#   [ 385.663429] qtmir.sensor: PlatformScreen[0x5e3d6b5eb0]::customEvent() - new orientation=Qt::InvertedLandscapeOrientation
#
#   [ 404.197702] qtmir.sensor: ...::customEvent() - unknown orientation.
#   [ 115.332982] / [ 115.587090] / [ 2218.837162] / [ 2568.911397] / [ 4542.555712] /
#   [ 4907.548381] / [ 5993.785854] ...
#
#   and, read over D-Bus while the phone lay flat and screen up:
#   $ gdbus call --system ... Get local.OrientationSensor orientation
#   (<(uint64 4907939888, uint32 6)>,)
#
# Three things follow, and they are the whole point of this log:
#   (1) the steady state is "6" -> Qt FaceUp(5) -> "unknown orientation." -> the screen
#       is left alone. That is not a fault and it is not an inversion: 6 is the correct
#       face-up value (section 4) and ignoring the face states is what qtmir does by
#       design (section 5). So the flat-phone value can never move the screen.
#   (2) the one change ever adopted in that boot was InvertedLandscape(8), which section
#       6 says comes from sensorfw RightUp(2) -- "the device's right side is up". Whether
#       that was true of the phone at second 385 is not recorded; what IS recorded is
#       that nothing was adopted afterwards.
#   (3) therefore the earlier reading of these same lines -- "the shell re-reads the
#       cached 6 and adopts it as landscape" (docs 70 section 5, 71 section 5) -- cannot
#       be right: 6 is the one value that provably does nothing.

